    "final_text": "I've built the CSV parser and serializer with no dependencies. The full gate passed: 335 tests with 0 failures across five test files, plus a syntax check on all 11 files, run through `dx run-gate --name full-gate`.\n\n**What's there**\n- `src/csv.js` (CommonJS) exports exactly `parse`, `createParser`, `stringify` and `CsvError`. The code behind it is split across `src/parser.js`, `src/stringify.js`, `src/options.js` and `src/errors.js`.\n- `parse` is literally one `write` followed by `end` on the streaming parser. So `parse` and `createParser` can't disagree on records or on error `line`/`column`.\n- The streaming parser keeps only the field and record it's currently reading, and reads each chunk once. A 13 MB file takes about 80 ms whether it arrives whole or in 4 KB chunks.\n- `package.json` has `npm test` (`node --test`) and `npm run lint` (a small syntax check, since there are no dependencies). `README.md` covers the API, error codes and the choices below.\n\n**How it's checked**\n- **Splits:** a set of valid and invalid inputs is fed through `createParser` split at every single position, every pair of positions, and one character per chunk. Each result, including every error's position, must match `parse`.\n- **Round trip:** 7,200 seeded random round trips cover 6 delimiters, both line endings and both `skipEmptyLines` settings. Each goes through `parse` and through chunked `createParser`.\n- **Speed:** a 300k-character field fed one character per `write` must finish quickly, so a parser that re-reads earlier input would fail.\n- **The tests catch real bugs:** I broke the code in four places (CRLF across chunks, line counting inside quotes, the `\"\"` rule, the BOM rule). Every break made tests fail.\n\n**Choices where the spec was silent or open** (all listed in the README)\n- **Delimiter width:** it must be one UTF-16 code unit, so an emoji delimiter throws a `TypeError`.\n- **BOM and columns:** a leading BOM isn't counted, so `'\\uFEFF\"abc'` reports the unclosed quote at column 1.\n- **Duplicate header timing:** it's reported as soon as the repeated field ends. So `a,a,\"b` reports the duplicate at column 3, not the unclosed quote after it.\n- **Header with no rows:** `stringify([], { header })` writes the header line rather than `''`. That output parses back to `[]` in header mode.\n- **One of my own additions to your quoting rules:** `stringify` quotes the first field if the output would otherwise start with a BOM. Without this, `parse` would drop the BOM and the round trip would lose data.\n- **`null` for options:** `null` or the wrong type for an option throws a `TypeError`. Only `undefined` means \"use the default\".\n- **Extra property:** `CsvError` also has a `code` property (`UNCLOSED_QUOTE`, `UNEXPECTED_CHARACTER`, `DUPLICATE_HEADER`, `FIELD_COUNT_MISMATCH`).\n\n**Git**\n- The work is in three commits on a new branch, `feat/csv-parser`. Nothing is pushed because the repo has no remote.\n- The commit hook required conventional-commit messages and a `Co-Authored-By: Dex` trailer, so I used those.\n- I left `CLAUDE.md` untracked, since it isn't mine.\n- The `humanizer` skill that `CLAUDE.md` asks for isn't installed here, so I edited the README by hand.",
